.TH TWK 1 "May 2026" "twk 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
twk \- track time worked against Azure DevOps work items
.PP
.nf
  _________      __   _____   ____  __.
 /\\__  ___/\\    /  \\ /  _  \\ |    |/ _|
 \\/_/  \\   \\/\\/  /  /  /_\\  \\|      <
    |   |  \\    /  /    |    \\    |  \\
    |___|   \\/\\/   \\____|__  /____|__ \\
                           \\/        \\/
     Task Worked and Committed
.fi
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B twk
.I command
.RI [ task ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B twk
(Task Worked and Committed) is a CLI tool for tracking time against
Azure DevOps work items from the terminal. Start, pause, and end
timing sessions locally, then commit the accumulated hours back to
your board in one push.
.PP
Multiple work items can be tracked in parallel for easy context switching.
.SH OVERVIEW
.B twk
has two distinct ways of writing to Azure DevOps:
.TP
.B "Commit cycle (deferred)"
.BR start ", " pause ", " end ", " undo ", " cancel
only touch local session files.
.B commit
is the single point at which accumulated hours are PATCHed to AzDO.
This makes the time-tracking flow safe to iterate on offline:
.B status
previews what
.B commit
will push, and
.BR undo / cancel
let you fix mistakes before any of it leaves your machine.
.TP
.B "Immediate AzDO actions"
.BR done " and " assign
PATCH AzDO state and assignee respectively as soon as you invoke
them — no staging, no
.BR commit ,
no twk-side undo. The
.B \-\-state
flag on any time-tracking command also fires an immediate state
PATCH alongside the local session change.
.TP
.B "Read-only"
.BR list ", " show ", " users ", " status ", " pull ", " init
either don't write to AzDO at all or only refresh local caches.
.B init
writes the local config file and tests AzDO connectivity once.
.B status
is local-first; it reads cached titles and only contacts AzDO
when invoked with
.BR \-\-with\-existing .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init \fR[\fB\-\-global\fR]
Configure the Azure DevOps connection. Prompts for organisation, project,
team, personal access token, and time tracking field names.
.IP
By default, writes a project-local config to
.I .twk/config
in the current directory. When twk runs, it walks up from the current
directory looking for
.IR .twk/config ;
if none is found, it falls back to the global config at
.IR ~/.config/twk/config .
Use
.B \-\-global
to write directly to the global config instead.
.IP
If the project-local config is created inside a git repository, twk
appends
.I .twk/
to
.I .gitignore
(creating the file if needed) to keep your Personal Access Token out
of version control.
.TP
.B start \fR[\fItask\fR] [\fB\-\-state\fR \fIstate\fR]
Start timing a work item. If the item is paused, resumes it instead.
Use
.B \-\-state
to set the work item state in Azure DevOps (e.g. Doing, Active).
When
.I task
is omitted, the interactive picker lists current sprint items with
already-running sessions hidden. Items with a paused session show
.I "paused HH:MM:SS"
to the right of their title; the AzDO assignee
.RI ( displayName )
is shown in a column at the end of each row. Titles are truncated
to 40 characters and assignee names to 15 characters, both with
.IR ... .
.TP
.B pause \fR[\fItask\fR] [\fB\-\-state\fR \fIstate\fR]
Pause an active timing session. Elapsed time is preserved.
Use
.B \-\-state
to set the work item state in Azure DevOps (e.g. Paused).
When
.I task
is omitted, the interactive picker only lists currently running
sessions; a single match is selected automatically.
.TP
.B end \fR[\fItask\fR] [\fB\-\-state\fR \fIstate\fR]
End a timing session. The item can be running or paused.
Use
.B \-\-state
to set the work item state in Azure DevOps (e.g. Done).
When
.I task
is omitted, the interactive picker only lists running or paused
sessions; a single match is selected automatically.
.TP
.B done \fR[\fItask\fR] [\fB\-\-state\fR \fIstate\fR]
Set the work item state in Azure DevOps without affecting any active
timer session. Uses the configured done state by default, or a specific
state via
.BR \-\-state .
.TP
.B assign \fR[\fItask\fR] [\fIuser\fR] [\fB\-\-me\fR] [\fB\-\-all\fR]
PATCH
.I System.AssignedTo
on the work item. The
.I task
argument supports the usual resolution modes (numeric ID, title
search, or interactive picker when omitted).
.I user
must be an email address, display name, or unique name that
Azure DevOps can resolve. When omitted, an interactive user
picker is shown — sprint-scoped by default.
.IP
.B \-\-me
assigns the current PAT owner, derived from
.IR /_apis/connectionData .
Cannot be combined with an explicit user or with
.BR \-\-all .
.IP
.B \-\-all
opens the user picker against the org-wide Graph API rather
than the sprint. Useful for assigning someone not yet on a
sprint item. Requires the PAT to have
.B "Graph (Read)"
scope in addition to
.BR "Work Items (Read & Write)" .
Cannot be combined with an explicit user.
.IP
With both task and user explicit, no pickers are invoked. If
user resolution fails, the PATCH fails and the work item is
left unchanged. Read-only against local sessions; modifies
AzDO state only.
.TP
.B reset \fR[\fItask\fR] [\fB\-y\fR | \fB\-\-yes\fR]
PATCH the configured time field on the work item to 0. The field
is whichever
.B "twk commit"
would write to (Task vs Feature resolved per item type from
.BR "twk init" ).
Destructive: whatever was logged on AzDO is wiped. By default
the command displays the current value and prompts
.RI ( "[y/N]" ).
.B \-y
or
.B \-\-yes
skips the prompt — use with care, intended for scripts. Local
sessions are not touched; an uncommitted session remains and
the next
.B "twk commit"
will push its hours onto the zeroed value.
.TP
.B comment \fR[\fItask\fR] [\fItext\fR | \fB\-\fR]
Post a comment to the work item's Discussion thread. Three input
modes for the comment body: an inline string as the second
positional argument; the literal
.B \-
to read from standard input (so
.B "cat notes.txt | twk comment 12345 \-"
works); or, with the body argument omitted, an interactive
.B $EDITOR
session
.RB ( $VISUAL
takes precedence; falls back to
.BR vi ).
Editor mode strips lines starting with
.B #
and aborts cleanly on empty content. The POST is immediate —
modifies AzDO state, no local file changes, no commit cycle.
Listed in the Discussion thread and visible to anyone with
read access to the work item.
.TP
.B undo \fR[\fItask\fR] [\fB\-\-state\fR \fIstate\fR]
Remove the most recent event (start, resume, pause, or end) from the
session file. Useful for recovering from a typo such as ending a
session when you meant to pause it. If removing the event leaves
the session empty, the session file is deleted. Use
.B \-\-state
to also update the work item state in Azure DevOps.
When
.I task
is omitted, the interactive picker only lists existing sessions; a
single match is selected automatically.
.TP
.B adjust \fR[\fItask\fR] [\fIamount\fR]
Manually adjust the recorded elapsed time on a session. Both
arguments are optional.
.I amount
must be a signed duration:
.BR + \fIduration\fR " (add), " \- \fIduration\fR " (subtract), or"
.BR = \fIduration\fR
(set absolute). Duration is one of
.IR Nh ", " Nm ", " Ns
or a combination
.RI ( "1h30m" ", " "1h30m45s" )
or a decimal hour value
.RI ( "2.5h" ).
The adjustment is appended to the session file as
.IB adjust| signed_seconds
so the audit trail is preserved and
.B "twk undo"
pops the adjustment as the last event.
.IP
When
.I task
is omitted, the interactive picker lists existing sessions. When
.I amount
is omitted,
.B twk
prompts for it; an empty response cancels cleanly. Single-arg
disambiguation: a leading
.BR + ", " \- ", or " =
makes the arg an amount, otherwise a task. Subtractions that
would make the total negative are rejected. Examples:
.BR "twk adjust 12345 +30m" ,
.BR "twk adjust +30m" ,
.BR "twk adjust 12345" ,
.BR "twk adjust" .
.TP
.B cancel \fR[\fItask\fR] [\fB\-\-state\fR \fIstate\fR]
Discard an uncommitted local session without pushing anything to
Azure DevOps. The session file is archived to
.I ~/.local/share/twk/sessions/cancelled/
for audit. Use
.B \-\-state
to also revert the work item state in Azure DevOps.
When
.I task
is omitted, the interactive picker only lists existing sessions; a
single match is selected automatically.
.TP
.B list \fR[\fB\-i\fR | \fB\-\-interactive\fR] [\fB\-\-sort=\fR\fIcol\fR]
List every work item in the current sprint as a single-row table
summary (columns: ID, Title, State, Pri, Est, Done, Assigned) with
the work item's description rendered as an indented sub-line beneath
each row. Layout mirrors
.B "twk status"
for consistency. Titles are truncated to 32 characters and assignee
names to 15 characters with
.IR ... ;
descriptions are HTML-stripped, whitespace-collapsed, and truncated
to 240 characters with
.IR ... .
Missing values render as
.IR - .
The "Done" column comes from the AzDO time field configured in
.B "twk init"
and is resolved per item type (Task vs Feature). The "Assigned"
column is read from
.IR System.AssignedTo.displayName .
.IP
With
.B \-i
or
.BR \-\-interactive ,
the list opens in fzf with a preview pane showing the full
description and metadata for whichever row is highlighted. On
enter, the selected work item ID is printed to stdout. Requires
fzf.
.IP
With
.BI \-\-sort= col
the items are sorted by the named column before rendering.
Valid columns:
.IR id ", " title ", " state ", " pri ", " est ", " done ", " assigned .
Prefix with
.B \-
for descending order
.RI ( --sort=-done
puts the most-worked-on items first). Missing values sort to
the end either way.
.IP
Output is piped through
.B "less -FRX"
when stdout is a TTY (set
.I TWK_NO_PAGER=1
to opt out, or
.I "PAGER=''"
to disable; respects an explicit
.IR $PAGER ).
.B less \-F
skips paging when output fits on one screen, so short lists are
unchanged. If
.B less
is not installed, output falls through to
.BR cat .
The same pager wrapping applies to
.B "twk show"
and
.BR "twk status" .
.TP
.B show \fR[\fItask\fR] [\fB\-\-discussion\fR]
Print one work item's full metadata and description as a single
labelled block: type, state, priority, assignee, estimate, time
taken, iteration path, and the full description (HTML-stripped,
whitespace-collapsed, line-wrapped to 78 characters - no
truncation). Use when
.B "twk list"
has truncated a description and you need to read it in full.
The
.I task
argument supports the same resolution modes as the session
commands (numeric ID, title search, or omitted for the
interactive picker).
.IP
With
.BR \-\-discussion ,
the AzDO Discussion thread (comments) is appended after the
metadata block in chronological order. Each comment renders as
.IR "[YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM] author:"
followed by the HTML-stripped body wrapped to 76 characters
with a two-space indent.
.TP
.B users \fR[\fB\-\-all\fR]
List unique users from the current sprint (default) or the entire
Azure DevOps organisation
.RB ( \-\-all ).
Default mode aggregates
.I System.AssignedTo
across the iteration's items and prints a three-column table:
ID (AzDO user UUID), Username
.RI ( displayName ),
and Email
.RI ( "uniqueName / UPN" ).
Useful for picking the right identifier to hand to
.BR "twk assign" .
Sprint-scoped — team members with no sprint assignments don't
appear. Uses the existing
.B "Work Items"
PAT scope.
.IP
With
.BR \-\-all ,
the same table is populated from the AzDO Graph API. Returns
every user in the org. Requires the PAT to have
.B "Graph (Read)"
scope in addition to the standard
.BR "Work Items (Read & Write)" .
Group entries are filtered out — only individual users appear.
.TP
.B log \fR[\fB\-\-days=\fR\fIN\fR | \fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-\-by-id\fR]
Browse the history of committed time entries. Reads
.I ~/.local/share/twk/sessions/committed/
and renders a chronological view (default: last 7 days, grouped
by date with newest first; each row leads with the commit time as
.IR HH:MM ).
With
.BR \-\-by-id ,
each entry shows the full datetime
.RI ( "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM" )
inline. Commit timestamps come from the archived filename; titles
come from the
.I .meta
sidecar (when present, otherwise
.I "(no title cached)" ).
.IP
.BI \-\-days= N
overrides the default 7-day window.
.B \-\-all
shows every committed session ever.
.B \-\-by-id
groups by work item ID with per-ID subtotals instead of grouping
by date. The grand total appears at the bottom in either layout.
.IP
Local-only: no network, no AzDO contact. Output auto-pages via
.BR "less -FRX" .
.TP
.B pull
For every uncommitted session, re-fetch the work item's title and
type from Azure DevOps and rewrite the
.I .meta
sidecar. Useful after starting sessions offline (which leaves
the title as
.IR "(no title cached)" )
or when work items have been renamed. Best-effort: per-session
failures are reported but do not abort the loop.
.TP
.B status \fR[\fB\-\-with-existing\fR]
Print the active config path and scope, followed by every uncommitted
time entry with its cached title (truncated to 40 chars), current
state, elapsed time, and decimal hours. Titles are cached on first
.B "twk start <id>"
to a sidecar
.I .meta
file, so
.B status
itself never hits the network by default.
.IP
With
.B \-\-with-existing
the command issues one batched call to the AzDO work-items endpoint
to read the current time field on every uncommitted work item, then
appends two columns:
.B "+ AzDO"
(the current value in the work item) and
.B "= Total"
(the value that would land on the next
.BR "twk commit" ).
Cells display
.I ?
if the lookup fails for any reason; the command itself does not fail.
.TP
.B commit \fR[\fB\-\-dry-run\fR]
Push accumulated time to the configured time field on each work item in
Azure DevOps. The target field depends on work item type (Task vs Feature)
as configured during init. Time is additive. Running sessions are skipped.
.IP
With
.BR \-\-dry-run ,
print the per-session projection (existing AzDO value, hours to add,
post-commit total) without writing anything. Sessions are not archived
and remain uncommitted. AzDO is still read to compute the projection;
only writes are suppressed.
.TP
.B version \fR(also \fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR)
Print the version number. The flag forms
.B \-v
and
.B \-\-version
work as top-level invocations
.RB ( "twk -v" ", " "twk --version" )
and produce identical output to the
.B version
subcommand.
.TP
.B help
Print usage information.
.SH TASK ARGUMENT
The
.I task
argument supports three resolution modes:
.TP
.I 12345
A numeric value is treated as a direct work item ID.
.TP
.I "partial title"
A string is matched case-insensitively against work item titles in the
current sprint. If multiple items match, you are prompted to choose.
.TP
(omitted)
When no argument is given, an interactive picker is shown. If
.B fzf
is installed it is used for fuzzy selection; otherwise a numbered list
is displayed.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B twk
supports two configuration scopes: project-local and global.
.PP
.B Project-local
configs live at
.I .twk/config
relative to a project root. On every command, twk walks up from
the current working directory and uses the first
.I .twk/config
it finds. This lets you keep separate Azure DevOps connections per
project (for example, different organisations or different time
fields).
.PP
.B Global
configs live at
.IR ~/.config/twk/config .
This config is used when no project-local config is found by walking
up from the current directory.
.PP
Run
.B twk init
to create a project-local config in the current directory, or
.B twk init \-\-global
to create the global config. Both files store your Azure DevOps
organisation, project, team, personal access token, and the field
names used for time tracking (which may differ between Tasks and
Features). The config directory is set to mode 700 and the config
file to mode 600.
.PP
When a project-local config is created inside a git repository,
.B twk
appends
.I .twk/
to
.I .gitignore
(creating the file if needed) to keep your Personal Access Token
out of version control.
.PP
Your PAT requires the
.B "Work Items (Read & Write)"
scope. Generate one at:
.PP
.RS
https://dev.azure.com/{your-org}/_usersettings/tokens
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ./.twk/config
Project-local Azure DevOps connection settings. Discovered by walking
up from the current working directory; takes precedence over the
global config.
.TP
.I ~/.config/twk/config
Global Azure DevOps connection settings. Used when no project-local
config is found.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/twk/sessions/<id>.session
Active time tracking session files. Each file contains append-only
events in the format
.BR event|timestamp .
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/twk/sessions/<id>.meta
Sidecar JSON file containing the cached work item title and type.
Written on first
.B "twk start <id>"
and consumed by
.B "twk status"
so that command stays offline.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/twk/sessions/committed/
Archived sessions that have been committed to Azure DevOps.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/twk/sessions/cancelled/
Archived sessions that were discarded with
.BR "twk cancel" .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TWK_NO_PAGER
When set (any non-empty value), disables the auto-pager for
.BR list ,
.BR show ,
and
.BR status .
.TP
.B PAGER
The pager used for long output from
.BR list ,
.BR show ,
and
.BR status .
Defaults to
.I "less -FRX"
when unset, or
.B cat
if
.B less
isn't installed. An empty value disables paging entirely.
.PP
All Azure DevOps configuration is stored in the config file
.RI ( "twk init" ).
.SH DEPENDENCIES
.B curl
(API requests),
.B jq
(JSON parsing),
.B bc
(decimal hour calculations). Optional:
.B fzf
(interactive picker),
.B git
(auto-appends
.I .twk/
to
.I .gitignore
on project-local
.B "twk init"
inside a git repo; otherwise twk prints a manual reminder).
.SH EXAMPLES
Track a single work item from start to finish:
.PP
.RS
.nf
twk start 12345
twk pause 12345
twk start 12345
twk end 12345
twk commit
.fi
.RE
.PP
Track multiple items in parallel:
.PP
.RS
.nf
twk start 12345
twk start 67890
twk pause 12345
twk end 67890
twk status
twk commit
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use title matching:
.PP
.RS
.nf
twk start "login bug"
twk end "login bug"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Recover from mistakes before committing:
.PP
.RS
.nf
twk undo 12345
twk cancel 12345
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Error (missing dependency, invalid arguments, API failure, or no
active session).
.TP
.B 130
Cancelled by the user via Ctrl+C. The trap on
.B SIGINT
prints
.I "Cancelled."
on stderr and exits cleanly so an interrupted picker or prompt
doesn't surface as a stack trace.
